## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design an incremental data structure for computing a non-trivial graph statistic under streaming batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental degree-based metrics
- Amortized analysis for batch/streaming updates
- Space-time trade-offs in dynamic graph algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
let deg = Array(n).fill(0);
let P = 0;

function addEdge(u, v) {
  const du = deg[u], dv = deg[v];
  P += 2 * (du + dv);
  deg[u] = du + 1;
  deg[v] = dv + 1;
}

function processBatch(edges) {
  edges.forEach(([u, v]) => addEdge(u, v));
  return P;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge deletions?
- How does multi-graph handling (parallel edges) affect this approach?
- What are the time and space complexities compared to recomputing from scratch?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a streaming heavy-hitters approach with bounded memory and clear trade-offs. It focuses on a practical, beginner-friendly variant of the classic Space-Saving problem, tying implementation to batch processing and real-time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Space-Saving / heavy-hitters
- Fixed-memory streaming
- Per-element vs per-batch costs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for Space-Saving with K counters
class Counter { constructor(id, count) { this.id = id; this.count = count; } }
let counters = new Map();
function update(e){
  if (counters.has(e)) { counters.get(e).count++ }
  else {
    if (counters.size < K) { counters.set(e, {count:1}) } 
    else { // replace min
      let minEntry = null; let minCount = Infinity;
      for (let [id, c] of counters) { if (c.count < minCount) { minCount = c.count; minEntry = id; } }
      counters.delete(minEntry);
      counters.set(e, {count: minCount + 1});
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust if the batch size B varies?
- How would you guarantee no false positives beyond a fixed error margin?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of streaming data structures and complexity trade-offs for maintaining order statistics under batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Running median via two heaps
- Per-element insert cost and batch amortization
- Memory growth with total elements

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianMaintainer {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();
    this.high = new MinHeap();
  }
  add(x) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || x <= this.low.peek()) this.low.push(x);
    else this.high.push(x);
    // rebalance
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) this.low.push(this.high.pop());
  }
  median() {
    if (this.low.size() === this.high.size()) return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
    return this.low.peek();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does varying batch size B affect latency and memory?
- How would you extend to handle sliding windows or duplicates efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Realistic scenario: social/ride networks grow incrementally; counting triangles relates to clustering.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental triangle counting; degree-based orientation; amortized analysis; sparse graphs; memory efficiency.

## Code Example
```javascript
function addBatch(G, batch){
  // G stores oriented adj lists and degree
  for(const [u,v] of batch){
    if(G.deg[u] > G.deg[v] || (G.deg[u] === G.deg[v] && u > v)) swap(u,v);
    // ensure oriented edge u->v
    G.adj[u].push(v);
    const common = intersect(G.adj[u], G.out[v]);
    G.triangles += common.length;
  }
}
```

